>[1] 2501

>[marc@localhost /usr]$ /usr/splus6/java/jre/bin/java: /usr/bin/cut:  No

>such file

>`/usr/splus6/java/jre/bin/i386/native_threads/java:  error while loading

>shared libraries:  libjvm.so: cannot load shared object file:  No such

>file or directory.

Thanks very much to Chuck Taylor and Krista Kilmer, both from Insightful, 
who provided the two essential pieces of information needed to get SPlus 6 
running under RH 7.1 Linux:

Red Hat decided to move the location of the program cut from \usr\bin to 
\bin.  So, I had to copy cut from \bin to \usr\bin.  That took care of the 
first problem.

Krista explains the second problem as:

"Apparently there is a "bugglet" in the current version of Splus 6
that causes a java "hang" under certain conditions.  This will be fixed
with the next update for version 6.0.  Until then, the following works
for me.  Edit the script file you use to start Splus.  Mine is located
at /usr/local/bin/Splus.  Add the following two lines to the top of that
file:

LD_ASSUME_KERNEL="2.2.5"
export LD_ASSUME_KERNEL

With these corrections, Splus seems to be working fine with RedHat 7.1."

I made the two recommended changes and all appears to be right with SPlus 6 
and RH 7.1.

From sebuttre at nps.navy.mil  Tue Sep  4 16:40:09 2001
From: sebuttre at nps.navy.mil (Buttrey, Samuel)
Date: Tue, 04 Sep 2001 09:40:09 -0700
Subject: Summary: Bug in symbols() with matrix argument
Message-ID: <21255707C24105438C5BEA7061D33228332F20@essex.ad.nps.navy.mil>

Hi. I wrote about a bug in symbols() when passed a matrix argument (it
doesn't work). Bill Dunlap of Insightful kindly wrote back and told me that
it is indeed a bug. Symbols will, he writes, "[work] with separate x,y
arguments or a list(x=,y=) argument but not with matrices or complex
numbers."

 No worries: I'm happy to send the two columns of my matrix as separate
arguments. Bill also offered this workaround:

"To work around this define one of the following new functions, depending on
whether you are using Splus2000 (or anything before that) or Splus5.0 (or
after).

Splus2000: my.symbols <- function(x, ...)marks.xy("symbols")

Splus6.0 : my.symbols <- function(x, y, ...)
        {
                .FDUMMY = function(x, y, ...)
                .Internal(xysort("zsymbo", x = x, y = y, ...),
"call_S_Version2")
                if(missing(y))
                        xyCall(x,  , .FDUMMY, ...)
                else xyCall(x, y, .FDUMMY, ...)
        }

The Fortran code underlying symbols() doesn't pull apart matrices or complex
numbers, just lists, so it requires the marks.xy or xyCall function to do
that.  You can see the same functions called for any of the other functions
that take x, y arguments in that style."

Many thanks to Bill D.,

Sam Buttrey

