Subject: Re: Error in integrate function
In-Reply-To: <7885CDD53840874980B0409FD6E3F6822E52CD@med-core05.med.wayne.edu>
Message-ID: <15104802790BD411A2C100D0B73EA33C01B4538D@remailnt3-re01.westat.com>

i think you have created an integrand that fools integrate() into thinking
it has converged when it hasn't even gotten close to the heart of the
matter.  your function is zero on a long interval.  you know your function
should be zero between -cut and cut (where you have defined cut to be
qnorm(percentile, mu, sigma).  if you integrate over the interval (cut,inf)
(and multiply by two) instead of on the interval (-inf,inf), i think
integrate() will handle your problem.

just for grins i wrote a function that integrates over a specified range and
i think it shows you have a problem integrating from -inf to inf when the
percentile reaches 0.90.  i think that as the percentile approaches 0.90 the
cut value you are calculating is moving far enough out (around 39.44) that
the integration method is missing most of the positive stuff and
concentrating it's mass where the function is zero.  because the function is
so dead zero on this interval and the interval is pretty wide, integrate()
thinks it has converged.

i wrote the integral as twice the integral from 0 to inf instead of -inf to
inf just to show there is a not a problem with negative values.  if you
don't want to do it this way integrate from -inf to cut and then from cut to
inf.

one other minor point.  instead of typing out the density for the normal
distribution i used the function dnorm().  it might have some internal
numerical advantages as well as saving typing.    

> #
# a test function that integrates over the range from x.lower to x.upper
# note that the originally specified integral was from -Inf to Inf
# it can be obtained as twice the integral from 0 to Inf
#
test.int <- function(x.cut, mu, sigma, x.lower, x.upper
        )
{
        integrand <- function(x, x.cut, mu, sigma)
        {
                ifelse(abs(x) <= x.cut, 0, 1) * ((abs(x
                        ) - x.cut)^2) * dnorm(x, mu, 
                        sigma)
        }
        integrate(integrand, lower = x.lower, upper = 
                x.upper, x.cut = x.cut, mu = mu, sigma
                 = sigma)$integral
}
> mu <- 0
> sigma <- 30   #
#
#this shows the problem happens for 0.90
#the zero part is the integral from 0 up to the cutoff point
#the positive part is the integral from the cutoff point to Inf
#the integral labelled bad is from 0 to Inf
#
> for(j in 1:5) {
#set the quantile value 
        cutoff <- 0.9 - (5 - j)/40
        x.cut <- qnorm(cutoff, mu, sigma)
        cat("\ncutoff =", cutoff, " zero part =", 2 * 
                test.int(x.cut, mu, sigma, 0, x.cut), 
                " positive =", 2 * test.int(x.cut, mu, 
                sigma, x.cut, Inf), " bad =", 2 * 
                test.int(x.cut, mu, sigma, 0, Inf))
}
