From lambert.winifred at ensco.com  Fri Sep  7 18:34:43 2001
From: lambert.winifred at ensco.com (Winifred Lambert)
Date: Fri, 07 Sep 2001 14:34:43 -0400
Subject: Summary: File/Object Locations
Message-ID: <D7E1E71C0658D4118C9D00104B6D756D139CDC@amu01>

Many, MANY thanks to all who replied to my question about moving objects
from one location to another: Jim Pratt, Phillip Stafford, Brian Ripley,
and Bill Dunlap.

First, Jim Pratt asked me to run objects() to see what objects were in
my working directory.  The function saw the objects with 'normal' names
(standard DOS 8.3 format), but not all the special S-Plus _XX files.
Then, Brian Ripley and Bill Dunlap explained the importance of copying
the associated _nonfi file with the objects, because it contains
essential S-PLUS mapping information about the objects.  Here are their
responses:

BILL DUNLAP
The files named __NUMBER (2 underscores followed by some digits) contain
Splus objects you made, but those objects have names than cannnot be
unambiguous DOS file names.  E.g., they may have capital letters, more
than one dot, or otherwise don't fit in the 8.3 lowercase format
expected.
The file ___nonfi (3 underscores) maps the Splus object names to the
file names for those __NUMBER files.  Hence you need to copy the
___nonfi
if you copy any __NUMBER files.

BRIAN RIPLEY:
I hope you moved the ___nonfi, or they are useless.  Deeper explanation.
On case-insensitive file systems, object names are mapped to __XX type
names, and the mapping is in ___nonfi.  The files themselves have no
meaning without reference to ___nonfi.
Object explorer/browsers need the summary files. __sum*.  They cna be
re-created.  Under some circumstances you need an empty _db3.1 file.

All respondents recommended moving the entire _data directory, not just
parts inside.  Jim Pratt and Phillip Stafford pointed out that the
official way to do this is to use data.dump and data.restore (look up in
help how to use these functions).

I should realize by now that S-PLUS has a function for just about any
basic thing you want to do.  I *did not* move the _nonfi file, and in my
wild flailing about to find my objects, I seem to have written over the
original one.  I think I will have to delete my _XX objects and start
over.  I only had about 30 objects and they were small and purely
exploratory in nature.  It seems like I have to make a big mistake
before I learn anything, but the damage was minimal this time.  DON'T
LET THIS HAPPEN TO YOU!

