Dear All,

SPLUS provides two log-likelihood scores for frailty model fitting. For
example, consider a gamma-distributed frailty model fit.

> fit <- coxph(Surv(data$time, data$status) ~ group +
       frailty(data$id, distribution="gamma"), eps=1e-3, iter.max=10)

The first likelihood is the usual partial likelihood with covariate and
frailty W_i included, however, the penalty term excluded. The values
of W_i that enter the likelihood as an offset term are their posterior
means.

> fit1$loglik[2]
[1] -668.9933

The other one is called EM loglikelihood, which can be seen in
summary(fit) and  extracted in the following way.

> summary(fit)
Call:
coxph(formula = Surv(data$time, data$status) ~ group + frailty(data$id,
        distribution = "gamma"), eps = 0.001, iter.max = 10)

