Subject: glm - statistical question
In-Reply-To: <409B678FC2558E4F925766483302C811238F7A@BUS1.bus.olemiss.edu>
Message-ID: <409B678FC2558E4F925766483302C811238F7F@BUS1.bus.olemiss.edu>

I sent yesterday a question regarding glm. I am including it in the bottom of the message.  I received detailed answer by Professor Venables and Professors Ripley pointed several mistakes and misundestandings on my party.  For anyone waiting for a summary of their replies, since I am still a bit confused, it will take me a while to post a detailed summary.  If anyone wants me to just forwards Professors Venables & Ripley responese, I will be happy post those replies. 

In order to test a submodel, Venables & Ripley, 2000, p 215, suggest a chi-square test. However, in many cases, the glm procedure will automatically change the number of observations in the submodel, and so my question is what is the proper test when this happens. An example should make things easier to understand.

Suppose the data is (y, x, , year), where y is coded 0/1. Also, assume that for some values of year,

all observations of y are the same.

reg1 <- glm(y ~ x, family = binomial(link = logit) )

The following runs as it should.

reg11 <- update(reg1,.~. + year)

g <- reg1$deviance - reg11$deviance

deg <- length(table(district)) - 1

1 - pchisq(g,df=deg)

However, if one runs

reg11 <- update(reg1,.~. + as.factor(year)),

Splus will estimate the model only for a subset of the data, the years for which y has values

of both 0 and 1. So, one can run the chisquare test, however, I don't think the answer will be meaningful.

One way around this is to run the first regression on the subset of the data which has variations in y for all existing values of year, however, this generates a correlation between the x & y variables. Are there other ways of solving the problem? Thanks.

