*** From Professor Ripley ****

On Sun, 9 Sep 2001, Boylan, Richard wrote:

> In order to test a submodel, Venables & Ripley, 2000, p 215, suggest a

That's S Programming, and nothing relevant on p. 215!

We don't suggest it on page 215 of MASS, either, as that reports the

theory, and we never suggest the procedure you have used.

> chi-square test. However, in many cases, the glm procedure will

> automatically  change the number of observations in the submodel, and so

> my question is what is the proper test when this happens.  An example

> should make things easier to understand.

I only think this happens if there are NAs involved.  Use of

na.action="" is certainly going to cause problems.

> Suppose the data is (y, x, , year), where y is coded 0/1.  Also, assume

> that for some values of year,

>

> all observations of y are the same.

>

> reg1 <- glm(y ~ x, family = binomial(link = logit) )

>

> The following runs as it should.

>

> reg11 <- update(reg1,.~. + year)

