*** From Professor Venables ***

> -----Original Message-----

> From: Boylan, Richard [mailto:rboylan at bus.olemiss.edu]

> Sent: Monday, 10 September 2001 6:40 AM

> To: s-news at biostat.wustl.edu

> Subject: [S] glm - statistical question

> 

> 

> In order to test a submodel, Venables & Ripley, 2000, p 215, suggest a

> chi-squared test. However, in many cases, the glm procedure will

> automatically change the number of observations in the submodel, and

> so my question is what is the proper test when this happens.  An

> example should make things easier to understand.

> 

> Suppose the data is (y, x, , year), where y is coded 0/1.  Also,

> assume that for some values of year,  

> all observations of y are the same.

> 

> reg1 <- glm(y ~ x, family = binomial(link = logit) )

> 

> The following runs as it should.

> 

> reg11 <- update(reg1,.~. + year)

> g <- reg1$deviance - reg11$deviance

> deg <- length(table(district)) - 1

> 1 - pchisq(g,df=deg)

That really is the long way round.  You could just do

> anova(reg1, reg11, test = "Chi")

> 

> However,  if one runs

>

> reg11 <- update(reg1,.~. + as.factor(year))

>

> Splus will estimate the model only for a subset of the data, the years

> for which y has values of  both 0 and 1.  

Actually, no.  It still uses all the data, but under some situations you

could get probabilities estimated as 0 or 1, in which case the linear

predictor is effectively (+ or -) infinite, and so a neat theory in the

logistic scale is not available.  This is unlikely, though, if you have a

regression variable, x, which is not constant over the classes of the year

factor.  If it is constant over the year some of the year classes you have

linear dependence in the predictors and the dimension of the covariate space

is not as large as you thought.  This can be thought of as a design failure

in some sense.

> So, one can run the

> chi-squared test, however, I don't think the answer will be meaningful. 

It is meaningful enough, but the asympototic theory on which it relies may

not apply.  Even this is not necessarily true, though: it may still be a

reasonable approximation.  Cases differ, of course.

> One way around this is to run the first regression on the subset of

> the data which has variations in y for all existing values of year,

> however, this generates a correlation between the x & y variables. Are

> there other ways of solving the problem?  Thanks.

There is a problem: you may not have enough data to answer the questions you

are trying to address.  Even exact tests are not going to help much, I

suspect.  As far as I know, the only way round this problem is the obvious

one: get more, and perhaps better targeted, data.

Sorry about that, but sometimes (as Freud used to say) a cigar is just a

cigar.

Bill Venables.

