Dear Mélanie,

 

Your understanding of the calculation of F in 
hierarchical design refers to the case of random effects design i.e. when both 
the higher hierarchical group and the subgroup are random factors. But they are 
two are other figures; namely, one factor fixed and the other random (or mixed 
effects design) and both factors are fixed (or fixed effects design). You may 
consult either Fundamentals of Experimental Design, 3rd edit. by Jerome L. Myers 
or Applied Linear Statistical Models, 2nd edit.by Neter, Wasserman, and Kutner 
(sorry, I am from old school; younger s-users may suggest recent references) for 
details. It is clear from the ANOVA table that you are running 

aov(depvar~popan/sexan,data) assuming the 
factors are fixed! If you're using the commands window, you may try this: 

>myrun<-aov(resp~popan/sexan,dataname)

>vec<-summary(myrun)[,3] 

>Fratio<-vec[1]/vec[2]

>summary(myrun)

>Fratio

 

Hopefully someone with master knowledge of the 
chapter 10 of "An Introduction to R" by Venables, Smith, and the R Development 
Core Team, will generously give an elegant and efficient version of this 
rudimentary program. It works though!

 

Ray.

