When considering nested function calls, one must care about
frames the function can match their arguments and value.

1."Function.correct" opens frame1 and assign x,y on frame1
2."discrim" opens frame2, and "discrim" has many nested
function call in it's definition.
3. The nested function in "discrim" opens frame3
...
It requires to evaluate the argument "subset" within some frame.
"seq" also has it's own frame and search "x" as far as possible, 
but "x" exists the frame that it cannot search for.

If you would define,

Function.correct<-function(x,y,z)
{
        x<-data.frame(x);y<-data.frame(y)
        yy <- y[seq(from=1, to=nrow(x), by=z),]
        dis<-discrim(formula = Col.A~Col.B+Col.C, data = yy, 
family = Classical("heteroscedastic"), prior = "proportional", 
predict="predictive")
        dispred<-predict.discrim(dis,method="predictive",newdata=x)
        return(dis,dispred)
}

It should work fine.

