From jmc at research.bell-labs.com  Thu Sep 13 18:55:37 2001
From: jmc at research.bell-labs.com (John Chambers)
Date: Thu, 13 Sep 2001 14:55:37 -0400
Subject: Re: S4 classes and methods. (Was: Re: dimnames in Sparc Splus 6.0)
References: <200109131632.NAA12608@tanner.math.unb.ca>
Message-ID: <3BA10129.250B3DF8@research.bell-labs.com>

David Smith and others from Insightful will continue to respond relative
to the details in S-Plus 6, but I do have to add a few comments in view
of some rather misleading statements.

We're probably stuck with the "S3 classes", "S4 classes" terminology,
but it's worth keeping in mind that the difference here is that in the
earlier version classes were not formally defined at all, and in the
later they are.

So the basic question for classes is whether one wants them to be
defined, or instead to have objects that can contain anything whatever
regardless of the class they seem to belong to.

There's no absolute argument on either side, in any of these questions. 
But the main argument in favor of having classes defined is that the
software can do more for users if it knows something about the objects
from that class.  For important classes of objects, it does seem to me
that users will benefit from this.

That's really the underlying point in most of the questions. 
Definitions of classes and generic functions can be used to validate
objects, check arguments, and generate software.  The result is often
fewer user errors.

Now, perhaps the owners of software written using the older system will
decide their designs are incompatible with the newer system.

As I understand David Smith's comments, the S-Plus implementation
doesn't prevent people from still using the older model if they want to.

But there's a lot of experience, not just with S (or even _mainly_ with
S), to support the idea that formal class and method definitions help in
writing clear and reliable software.  The specific style of S classes
and methods is particularly similar to CLOS and Dylan, but the slightly
different style of languages like Java and C++ still supports the basic
assertion as well.

I hope nothing in the S language is cast in stone.  If there are changes
that would improve the design, let's hear them.  It's a good time for
all users of S-Plus and R to discuss where the language should go.

To the extent that the current thread has been about substance, rather
than just feelings, there seem to be rather easy solutions to most of
the specifics, _if_ the package developer is willing to write a new
version in the new form.

For example, having miscellaneous "attributes" that vary from one object
to another in the same class.  There are several ways of doing this,
ranging from a class about which nothing is known to having a specific
"miscellaneous" slot.  But, again, if nothing is known about the class,
the software can't help to correct typos and other errors.  As a result,
there's a greater potential for wrong answers.  And wrong answers seem
the worst kind of software error.

Multiple inheritance.  Actually, the S4 model does allow multiple
inheritance in the usual sense of the term.  The difference is that the
inheritance is defined by the class definition, and doesn't vary
arbitrarily from one object to another from the same class.  (It's
harder to describe what your software does, in that case.)

Having different arguments for different methods.  Again, this is not
hard--having a "..." argument sets that possibility up.  But realize
that you pay here as well because the software may not catch misspelled
argument names, for example.

To repeat, I wouldn't want owners of large older systems to be forced to
change.  But I also wouldn't want the resistance of the owners of those
systems to deprive new user/programmers of a more powerful programming
environment.

My personal feeling is that S belongs now to its users.  We designed and
implemented several versions as part of statistics research at Bell
Labs.  Each of those responded to what we saw as useful new ideas for
statistical computing.  Now we're off doing other new things, with the S
language as one of the main tools.  The joint work in the omegahat
project has some examples (www.omegahat.org) that I think open up
exciting new possibilities.  For that work, the S3 approach to classes
and methods would not be suitable, in my opinion.

S-Plus from Insightful, and R from the R development team, are
implementations that differ in details from any of the versions out of
Bell Labs (and so they should).  I repeat, this is a good time for
positive suggestions for improvements, particularly if they could be
incorporated into both systems.

Positive suggestions, preferably.

Regards,
 John Chambers
