David,

You don't need to be forgiven for contrariness so don't worry.
My choice of the word "broken" could have been better.  Let's
call it "problematic".

I think the places where we have differing views relate to
- non-downward compatibility when adding attributes to
  simple objects
- the problem with multiple inheritance
- whether the new class system has resulted in advances in
  productivity of programmers, reliability of code, or new
  capabilities or efficiencies

All this being said, Splus 6 is working OK for me on Linux
except for a few annoying warning messages, after I've
overridden [.data.frame and [.factor and turned off a
couple of features in the Hmisc library for variables
with the "label" attribute.  I also have to run old
data frames through a function in Hmisc called
cleanup.import to get rid of secondary classes such
as "labelled", and run data frames through 
exportDataStripped before exporting them, so that
"label" attributes will not clobber the export function.

John Chambers wrote a very constructive note which I
appreciate.  I did consider some of the workarounds
which he mentioned but found that these caused a few
other problems.  For example, one of the workarounds,
if I understand it, involved putting the data in a slot
in a new "catch all" class of object and putting
attributes in another slot.  Tim Hesterberg did a lot
of work in writing this up nicely.  Ultimately I didn't
use it because it required demoting the data "down"
a level which would make the object incompatible with
several old functions.

All the best,

Frank
