From m1jjh00 at frb.gov  Thu Sep 13 21:03:38 2001
From: m1jjh00 at frb.gov (Jeffrey J. Hallman)
Date: Thu, 13 Sep 2001 17:03:38 -0400
Subject: Re: S4 classes and methods. (Was: Re: dimnames in Sparc Splus 6.0)
In-Reply-To: <3BA10129.250B3DF8@research.bell-labs.com>
References: <3BA10129.250B3DF8@research.bell-labs.com>
Message-ID: <200109132103.RAA22049@mrpmx1.rsma.frb.gov>

I've been writing S code for over ten years, and I find myself in agreement
with Rolf, Frank, and Terry.  The new system is too complicated and
too inflexible.  It's not enough to say that the old code will still run with
only minor modifications.  True, you can keep writing old-style code and
replace 'class' with 'oldClass' everywhere, and many things will work, but
then why bother with the new version at all?  Like Frank, after several months
of struggle converting my production code to Splus 6.0, I've given up and will
soon be porting to R.

After reading John Chamber's message to the list, I understand where he's
coming from, but I think it's the wrong place.  I don't think formality has
much place in an interactive, user friendly system.  We don't need a system
that is as inflexible and hard to program as CLOS, C++, and Java.  We need
(and had) a system that was more akin to a scripting language.  

It seems to me that the SV4 design tries to do way too much. Multiple
inheritance and multi-methods (dispatching on the classes of several
arguments, rather than just one) add needless complication.  Once you have
multi-methods, a method can no longer belong to just one class, so you need a
separate database that keeps track of what argument types can be used for each
argument of each method. Then you have to have a facility to let the
programmer override the default dispatch.  And so on, and so on, ad infinitum.
John is quite correct that SV4 resembles CLOS.  I would contend that design
complexity and all those damm parentheses are the reasons CLOS has never
really caught on.

I do think object-oriented programming really is the cat's meow, but SV4, like
CLOS, has made a hash of it.  If you want to see how nice an object
programming system can be, have a look at Smalltalk.  Several free and/or
noncommercial implementations are available.  My favorite is VisualWorks,
about which you can find more information at the VisualWorks Wiki site: 

http://wiki.cs.uiuc.edu/VisualWorks .

The only problem with VisualWorks is that it is fairly slow at floating point
arithmetic.  However, you can dynamically load C code to handle that, and I
have an elementary vector/matrix package on the Wiki that does just that.
Point your browser at http://wiki.cs.uiuc.edu/VisualWorks/Smallpack for
details.

I could probably be persuaded to participate in, or even lead, a project to do
statistics and econometrics in Smalltalk, if anyone else is interested.  

Jeff Hallman

>>>>> "JC" == John Chambers <jmc at research.bell-labs.com> writes:

  JC> David Smith and others from Insightful will continue to respond relative
  JC> to the details in S-Plus 6, but I do have to add a few comments in view
  JC> of some rather misleading statements.

  JC> We're probably stuck with the "S3 classes", "S4 classes" terminology,
  JC> but it's worth keeping in mind that the difference here is that in the
  JC> earlier version classes were not formally defined at all, and in the
  JC> later they are.

  JC> So the basic question for classes is whether one wants them to be
  JC> defined, or instead to have objects that can contain anything whatever
  JC> regardless of the class they seem to belong to.

  JC> There's no absolute argument on either side, in any of these questions.
  JC> But the main argument in favor of having classes defined is that the
  JC> software can do more for users if it knows something about the objects
  JC> from that class. For important classes of objects, it does seem to me
  JC> that users will benefit from this.

  JC> That's really the underlying point in most of the questions. Definitions
  JC> of classes and generic functions can be used to validate objects, check
  JC> arguments, and generate software. The result is often fewer user errors.

  JC> Now, perhaps the owners of software written using the older system will
  JC> decide their designs are incompatible with the newer system.

  JC> As I understand David Smith's comments, the S-Plus implementation
  JC> doesn't prevent people from still using the older model if they want to.

  JC> But there's a lot of experience, not just with S (or even _mainly_ with
  JC> S), to support the idea that formal class and method definitions help in
  JC> writing clear and reliable software. The specific style of S classes and
  JC> methods is particularly similar to CLOS and Dylan, but the slightly
  JC> different style of languages like Java and C++ still supports the basic
  JC> assertion as well.

  JC> I hope nothing in the S language is cast in stone. If there are changes
  JC> that would improve the design, let's hear them. It's a good time for all
  JC> users of S-Plus and R to discuss where the language should go.

  JC> To the extent that the current thread has been about substance, rather
  JC> than just feelings, there seem to be rather easy solutions to most of
  JC> the specifics, _if_ the package developer is willing to write a new
  JC> version in the new form.

  JC> For example, having miscellaneous "attributes" that vary from one object
  JC> to another in the same class. There are several ways of doing this,
  JC> ranging from a class about which nothing is known to having a specific
  JC> "miscellaneous" slot. But, again, if nothing is known about the class,
  JC> the software can't help to correct typos and other errors. As a result,
  JC> there's a greater potential for wrong answers. And wrong answers seem
  JC> the worst kind of software error.

  JC> Multiple inheritance. Actually, the S4 model does allow multiple
  JC> inheritance in the usual sense of the term. The difference is that the
  JC> inheritance is defined by the class definition, and doesn't vary
  JC> arbitrarily from one object to another from the same class. (It's harder
  JC> to describe what your software does, in that case.)

  JC> Having different arguments for different methods. Again, this is not
  JC> hard--having a "..." argument sets that possibility up. But realize that
  JC> you pay here as well because the software may not catch misspelled
  JC> argument names, for example.

  JC> To repeat, I wouldn't want owners of large older systems to be forced to
  JC> change. But I also wouldn't want the resistance of the owners of those
  JC> systems to deprive new user/programmers of a more powerful programming
  JC> environment.

  JC> My personal feeling is that S belongs now to its users. We designed and
  JC> implemented several versions as part of statistics research at Bell
  JC> Labs. Each of those responded to what we saw as useful new ideas for
  JC> statistical computing. Now we're off doing other new things, with the S
  JC> language as one of the main tools. The joint work in the omegahat
  JC> project has some examples (www.omegahat.org) that I think open up
  JC> exciting new possibilities. For that work, the S3 approach to classes
  JC> and methods would not be suitable, in my opinion.

  JC> S-Plus from Insightful, and R from the R development team, are
  JC> implementations that differ in details from any of the versions out of
  JC> Bell Labs (and so they should). I repeat, this is a good time for
  JC> positive suggestions for improvements, particularly if they could be
  JC> incorporated into both systems.

  JC> Positive suggestions, preferably.

  JC> Regards, John Chambers John M. Chambers jmc at bell-labs.com Bell Labs,
