From therneau at mayo.edu  Mon Sep 17 13:10:04 2001
From: therneau at mayo.edu (Terry Therneau)
Date: Mon, 17 Sep 2001 08:10:04 -0500
Subject: Date format, summary of responses
Message-ID: <200109171310.IAA18253@rocky.mayo.edu>

  I had asked how to produce the output format "10Mar1953" from the dates
functions, and recieved several responses.  (This is for the survival 
code, BTW).  

1. First, the encouraging part is that I hadn't missed something obvious
in the manual: dates() does not create this format.

2. Frank Harrell and David James pointed out that the out.format
argument in dates can be a function of your own devising, and both supplied
a simple example that pastes together the day, month and year.

3. The dates() function will produce the a delimited output such as
10-Mar-1953.  Paul Lin, Luciano Molinari, and Carlise Thacker all
provided functions that would remove the extra punctuation. These are
all interesting in their own right.

Thacker:
   takeout <- function(string) {
       do.call(function(...) paste(..., sep=""),
               unpaste(string, sep="-"))
        }
Unpaste() breaks the result into 3 strings, which are pasted back together
again.

Molinari:
   takeout <- function(string) {
        n <- nchar(string)
        temp <- substring(string, 1:n, 1:n)
        temp <- temp[temp != "-"]
        paste(temp, collapse="")
        }
The vector temp has each character of string as a separate element.  Throw
away the separators and collapse it.

Lin:
   takeout <- function(string) {
        for (i in 1:2) {
                where <- regexp('-', string)
                string <-  paste(substring(string, 1, where-1),
                                 substring(string, where+1), sep='')
                }
        }
The regexp function finds the location of the first "-", snip it out, then
do it again.

4. Chuck Taylor had the most interesting suggestion -- replace one of the
internal S functions (and he is from StatSci!).  The parse.format
function, which has no help page, appears to do the conversion of a date
to a character string.  He added "!" as a legal separator, but caused it
to map to "" as a print character.

 Thanks to everyone for their suggestions.

        Terry Therneau

