From Sicco.Schets at asml.com  Tue Sep 18 16:17:33 2001
From: Sicco.Schets at asml.com (Sicco Schets)
Date: Tue, 18 Sep 2001 18:17:33 +0200
Subject: Differences between UNIX and PC version of Splus
Message-ID: <3BA7739D.D54FED1D@asml.nl>

Hi,

I thought today to try a test of my Splus code, programmed under Splus6
for UNIX, and try it on the PC version (a trial version) to test
portability and speed.

Conclusions:
1) Not portable at all!!! A lot of warnings and errors in most basic
things, especially with coercion.
2) PC seems about 2-3 times faster (400MHz PC with 128MB versus and SUN
Ultra 60 with 512 MB).

Is there any easy way to port S code between PC and UNIX versions?

By the way, I have been using Splus (and R) for 2 years now (possibly a
total of 20.000 lines of code) on a frequent basis and I am getting more
and more annoyed at the fact that more than half the time spent on
programming is to make sure all your data objects remain the way you
want them. For instance, you could pass a data.frame from one function
to another and find that all kinds of attributes have been added or that
the mode of certain columns have changed etc etc. This is just one
example of the hassle one has to go through. Basically you must
constantly coerce from one type to another and many times even this is
not as you would expect.
Don't get me wrong. I really acknowledge and appreciate the incredible
power S (or R) can have, especially for exploratory analysis but also
for automation, but the fact that my programs sometimes have to turn
into incomprehensible code just because the program is full of what I
will refer to as 'coercion code', is starting to annoy me.

Of course I then wonder if it is not me that is causing this culprit of
code and annoyance. Possibly I have a lack of basic understanding of
Splus and its objects (although I have had official courses on the
matter), which causes me to have to build 'coercion code' around
everything I program.

I hope the latter is the case and that by giving me some basic rules I
can prevent many of these cases from occuring. The books provided with
Splus definitely don;t do it for me. I work at a competitive company.
where I cannot spend hours looking at nice books, as I would in my
student days.

Thanks for listening. I hope some of you can concur.

Thanks
Sicco

Sicco.Schets.vcf

